00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:56.960
So container network
security 101--

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.970
first, how is securing
containers and container

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:01.010
networking different than VMs?

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.140
Well, containers have a
different surface of attack

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.940
than virtual machines do.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.710
They are meant to
be smaller, meant

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:09.704
to reduce bloat images
that you deploy.

00:01:09.704 --> 00:01:11.120
For networking,
what that means is

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.036
that a lot of the plugins
and things like that

00:01:13.036 --> 00:01:14.134
should be smaller.

00:01:14.134 --> 00:01:15.800
It's the same idea,
you're going to want

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.660
to reduce the amount of things
that you add to your container

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.150
images so you have fewer
chance of vulnerabilities

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:21.980
in those containers.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:26.092
Service isolation--
containers and orchestrators

00:01:26.092 --> 00:01:28.050
like Kubernetes have some
built-in ways for you

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:29.466
to isolate your
different services

00:01:29.466 --> 00:01:32.330
and resources, including
storage, permission,

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:33.500
networking, et cetera.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.320
One of those things
are firewalls and then

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:36.380
network policies.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:39.770
Kubernetes has built-in
concepts to let

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:41.780
you have isolation
with firewalls

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:44.410
between different
containers in your network.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:47.260
Permissioning-- access
control on network resources

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.300
and other resources
allow delegation

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.860
of administrative
functions without having

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:53.402
potential compromise
or other issues.

00:01:53.402 --> 00:01:54.985
So you can have a
separation of duties

00:01:54.985 --> 00:01:56.464
between who sets
up your containers

00:01:56.464 --> 00:01:57.880
and your networking
versus who has

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.550
to use those containers
and that networking.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:03.950
And lastly, lifecycle--
containers, on average,

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:05.120
have a very short lifespan.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.720
Sysdif estimates that 95% of
containers live for less than a

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:08.630
week.

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:10.588
What that means from the
attacker point of view

00:02:10.588 --> 00:02:13.360
is that it's harder for someone
to persist in your network.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.820
But if they do persist
in your network,

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.136
and then you restart that
container or redeploy

00:02:18.136 --> 00:02:20.260
that container, you might
not have any forensics to

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.307
as to what happened
after the fact.

00:02:22.307 --> 00:02:23.890
This doesn't really
impact networking,

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:27.684
but might impact how you put
in place certain controls.

00:02:27.684 --> 00:02:30.100
MANJOT PAHWA: So what are the
different surface of attacks

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.630
we need to be worried about?

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:33.250
The first one, as
Maya mentioned,

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.030
is network resources.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.450
As an organization,
you might not

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:40.090
want critical network
resources like your lb rules,

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:44.140
your firewalls, your peerings,
to be under the control

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:46.280
of every single project admin.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.970
You might want to have
them be under the control

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:52.270
or be set up by network
and security admins.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:54.640
Second, private
addressing-- you might

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:57.100
want to ensure that your
entire deployment, including

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:59.260
your nodes, your
boards, et cetera are

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.790
within the secure
boundaries of your VPC.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:05.450
Moreover, you might want
to ensure that your control

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:08.890
pane is also isolated
from unauthorized access

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:10.390
over the internet.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:14.080
Third, service communication--
another thing to ensure around

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.020
the surface area of
attack can potentially

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:19.130
be service-to-service
communication.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:21.100
So you might want
to ensure or control

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.996
the access of which
services can can

00:03:22.996 --> 00:03:25.450
talk to which other
services through something

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:27.490
like network policy,
which enables

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:30.020
you to set up those rules.

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:32.440
Fourth, managed
services access--

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:35.470
when you're using a Kubernetes
or a GKE deployment,

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:39.880
chances are you're also using
a host of GCP-managed services,

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.910
like Container Registry, or
data Dataproc or BigQuery,

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:44.270
and several others.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:47.080
Another thing to ensure, or
another surface area of attack

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:51.010
to cover, is the communication
between your GKE deployments

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:54.800
and your managed services.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:57.820
So looking at network
security overall,

00:03:57.820 --> 00:03:59.930
the image in front
of you will tell you

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:01.940
where all those pieces fit in.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:03.890
So first and foremost,
your deployments

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:07.280
are within the secure
boundaries of a VPC network.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.520
You have your private
clusters, which

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.970
have no public IPs on
the nodes, and which

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:16.760
can securely communicate
to other clusters over VPC

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:17.870
firewalls.

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:21.260
Moreover, you have VPC service
controls to control access

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:23.720
to other managed services
like Container Registry

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.090
and BigQuery.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:28.610
On the L7
internet-facing side you

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:31.460
have Cloud Armor, an
Identity-Aware Proxy setup,

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:34.310
with your global load
balancer to control access

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:36.050
to your GKE deployments.

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:40.190
And you use master authorized
networks in your GKE clusters

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:45.590
to control unauthorized access
to your Kubernetes master.

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:47.510
Similarly, you
have network policy

00:04:47.510 --> 00:04:49.560
if you drill down
within a single cluster

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.320
for a setup for
service-to-service

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:53.780
communication.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:58.480
So let's talk about the network
security controls in detail.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:03.370
The first thing we are going
to talk about is Shared VPC.

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:05.890
So I spoke about how,
as an organization,

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:08.890
you want to delegate
administrative responsibilities

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:10.930
to your project
admins, but you don't

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:14.890
want your critical network
resources to be dealt by them.

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:18.370
You want them to be under the
network and security admins.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:20.770
Shared VPC enables
you to do that.

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:23.650
Private clusters help you
achieve network segmentation

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:26.260
by ensuring that your nodes are
within the secure boundaries

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:31.360
of your VPC and use
only RFC 1918 addresses.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.050
And moreover, for
private clusters,

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.190
another thing you can
potentially put in place

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:38.290
is master authorized networks.

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:40.190
A master authorized
networks puts

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:44.800
in an IP whitelist on your
Kubernetes or GKE master

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:48.220
and ensures that it's blocked
from unauthorized access

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:49.960
over the internet.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:56.200
Network policy helps you
segment microservices

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:59.810
by restricting which services
can talk which other services.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:03.880
Now, let's talk in more
detail about each of those.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:06.450
So I spoke briefly
about Shared VPC.

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:08.940
And Shared VPC is
what enables you

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:11.700
to map your organizational
topology to your network

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:12.820
topology.

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:16.050
So let's say you
have an infra team

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:19.710
and several apps teams
in your organization.

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:21.690
You want the apps
team project admins

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:24.360
to be able to create,
update, delete, and manage

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.790
those clusters seamlessly,
but you might not

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:30.270
want them to set up things
like org-level firewalls,

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:32.730
or you might not
want them to set up

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:36.010
lb rules, and a host
of other such things.

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:40.930
So in order to have
that model be present,

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:42.600
you can use Shared VPC.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:48.870
So you put your infrastructure
project in your host project,

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:51.450
and you have a shared VPC
within that host project.

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:56.340
Within the shared VPC you add
a host of service projects

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:00.600
which actually have your
app-specific clusters.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:03.240
This way you get security
and separation of rules,

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:05.490
your network and
security admins set up

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:08.010
the firewalls and not
the project admins,

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:10.440
and you get sharing
of common resources.

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:12.664
Because if, for example,
you have a pool of IP

00:07:12.664 --> 00:07:15.330
addresses that you want to share
across that shared VPC network,

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:17.100
you can actually achieve that.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:20.370
You get isolation
of data in workloads

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:23.520
by isolating the different
projects in the subnets.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.730
And you also get more
granular billing,

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:30.170
because you can actually
separate out the projects.

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:32.750
So Shared VPC deep dive--

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:34.990
as I mentioned, there
is a host project,

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:37.670
and within the host project
there's a shared VPC network.

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:40.980
The shared VPC network
itself can be divided

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:44.340
into a bunch of subnets.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:46.130
And then you give
your app projects

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:49.010
permissions over
a single subnet.

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:53.060
So you give them the
compute.network user permission

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:54.380
over a single subnet.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:58.590
And this way, your app teams
can actually create, manage,

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:00.290
and delete clusters.

00:08:00.290 --> 00:08:01.790
But if you want to
set up firewalls,

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:04.070
let's say, then you have
to go to the network admin.

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:06.020
So you talk to the
network admin and you

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:09.320
ask them to set up firewalls for
you in the Shared VPC network.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:12.350
This way, you get, as I
mentioned earlier, separation

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:14.210
and isolation of
rules, while still

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:16.190
enabling you to share
common resources

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:19.354
throughout your organization.

00:08:19.354 --> 00:08:20.770
Moving on to the
second one, which

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:23.620
is private deployments
with private clusters,

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:27.250
private clusters take your
nodes off the public internet

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:29.920
by removing all the public IPs.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:33.429
We used to initially
required the public IPs

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:37.120
in order to communicate
with your GKE master.

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:39.630
But that is no longer a
requirement with advancements

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:41.480
in our peering capabilities.

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:43.900
So now for your
private clusters,

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:46.270
the communication between
your master and your nodes

00:08:46.270 --> 00:08:49.630
is completely private
and within your VPC.

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:53.290
And moreover, if you create
a private cluster, by default

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:56.200
we enable master
authorized network,

00:08:56.200 --> 00:09:00.220
which default denies all
unauthorized access to your

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:02.770
to your GKE master.

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:06.160
Private clusters
help you simplify

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:08.740
the scope of some of the
compliance requirements

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:13.160
that you might have
for your applications.

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:16.020
As I said about master
authorized networks,

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:18.030
by default, if you
create a private cluster,

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:19.780
we enable master
authorize network,

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:23.250
and we highly recommend using
master authorized network along

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.290
with your private cluster.

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:28.770
If you want to enable certain
IP ranges on your on-premises

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:31.830
to have access to
your GKE master,

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:33.840
you can set up a master
authorized network

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:36.870
with some certain site
or ranges that you want

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:39.900
to whitelist over your master.

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:42.720
This way you have
a private cluster,

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:45.510
and you block unauthorized
access, and you allow only

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:48.390
authorized IP
addresses to access it.

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:51.570
A brief note about shared VPC--

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:54.930
so you cannot move from a
non-shared VPC to a shared VPC.

00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:57.780
You cannot update an existing
cluster in a non-shared VPC

00:09:57.780 --> 00:09:59.590
model to a shared VPC model.

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:03.780
Similarly, you cannot update
a cluster that is not private

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:04.920
to become private.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:06.980
But you can add master
authorized network

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:08.410
to an existing cluster.

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:11.700
So you might want to start off
with shared VPC model first

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:14.460
of all, then move on to
creating private clusters,

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:19.580
and then updating, removing
master authorized networks.

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:23.210
Looking deeper into how
to enable it in the UI,

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:28.550
you clearly see a
network security space

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:30.317
where you can enable
a private cluster.

00:10:30.317 --> 00:10:32.150
And the moment you
enable a private cluster,

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:35.360
you see that we have enabled
master authorized network,

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:38.760
where it says "Access master
using the external IP address."

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.600
So you can actually
add all trace networks

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:46.270
right underneath it, if
you want to, over there.

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:48.340
Network policy-- so
I've already mentioned

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:53.200
how as you develop a
microservices architecture

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:55.030
for your applications,
you might want

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:58.600
to restrict which services can
doctor which other services.

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:00.970
Earlier, when in
VM-based architectures,

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:03.250
how services were
isolated were usually

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:07.450
with a firewall or
network layer firewall,

00:11:07.450 --> 00:11:10.660
which was very typically a
physical device on the VM.

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:13.150
So a service was
isolated, let's say,

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:16.480
in a few VMs, which could only
talk to certain other VMs.

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:19.240
But that model doesn't work
when it comes to containers.

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:22.840
As Maya mentioned earlier,
the container lifecycle

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:24.950
is very short and
they're very dynamic.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:26.530
So they keep on
coming and going.

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:30.100
So the newer way to
actually control or restrict

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:32.920
service-to-service communication
and set access controls

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:35.170
on which service can talk
to which other service, you

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:37.870
want to use network policies.

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:41.250
Network policies enable
you to ensure that--

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:45.310
let's say you have a
compromised front end service.

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:46.810
If you have a
compromised front end,

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:49.210
you might want to ensure
that something as critical

00:11:49.210 --> 00:11:52.750
as your payments' backend
pods are not compromised.

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:55.540
So this way you can set
up network policy rules

00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:56.860
for your services.

00:11:56.860 --> 00:12:00.970
So, for example, in
this policy here, you

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:02.890
have ingress and
egress rules which

00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:05.470
can work on the basis
of port selector labels

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:07.300
that you might have
on your services,

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:11.150
or you can work on the
basis of IP address rules.

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:14.290
So you can add address ranges
that have access or don't

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:16.870
have access to your services.

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:23.470
Network policy is enforced in
nearly real-time and scales

00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:27.640
with your GKE
clusters seamlessly.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:30.910
Ingress integrations-- now,
another very critical aspect

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:34.179
of your deployment is
ingress, and the communication

00:12:34.179 --> 00:12:36.220
to the internet, and how
ingress actually sets up

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:37.360
your load balancer.

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:39.730
So we will talk about
a host of features

00:12:39.730 --> 00:12:43.240
you have available to yourself
to integrate with ingress.

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:45.460
The first one is
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:48.520
You can enable your employees
to work from untrusted networks

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:51.190
without the need of a VPN.

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:54.520
And moreover, you can give
them more granular access

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:56.650
than an all-or-nothing
access, which

00:12:56.650 --> 00:12:58.810
is typical of VPN connections.

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:02.170
Cloud Armor is our defense
against distributed

00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:05.020
denial-of-service
attacks engine.

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:07.840
You definitely want to have
some sort of DDoS protection

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:12.360
built-in into your load balancer
or some sort of DDoS protection

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:15.700
enabled for your deployments to
prevent from all sorts of edge

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:17.810
attacks from malicious actors.

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:19.720
Multiple TLS
certificate support--

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:23.980
so this is to enable
multiple SSL certificates

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:26.470
or multiple TLS certificates
for your deployments,

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:28.430
for every single
domain that you own.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:33.740
So the first one,
Identity-Aware Proxy,

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:36.440
which enables you
IAM-based access

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:39.540
control on per
application basis.

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:42.830
Identity-Aware Proxy acts as
a central authorization layer

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:44.600
for all your applications.

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:47.910
And you can control
who gets access

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:53.090
on an application-level basis
to which all internal apps

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:54.710
you might have,
instead of relying

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:57.620
on network level
firewalls or VPN,

00:13:57.620 --> 00:13:59.900
as I had mentioned earlier.

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:04.730
You see on the slide here how
to actually integrate with IAP.

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:08.600
So the first thing you define
is a BackendConfig object.

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:10.760
We introduced this
concept of a BackendConfig

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:15.890
object so that we could have
more GCP-specific annotations

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:19.100
for your GKE clusters.

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:22.100
The first thing you do is
define the BackendConfig object.

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:26.300
And then within the spec,
you set your IP settings.

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:28.790
The actual client, you
have to go ahead and create

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:30.980
as part of your IAP setup flow.

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:34.590
And then you just come back
here and add your secret name,

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:37.050
once you generate
the secret, as well.

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:41.270
And then once you've created
your BackendConfig object,

00:14:41.270 --> 00:14:43.280
you go back to your
service definition

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:46.700
and you actually add a
link within annotations

00:14:46.700 --> 00:14:49.880
for the service to your
BackendConfig object,

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:52.010
as mentioned here.

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:54.590
As I mentioned, IAP
performs authentication

00:14:54.590 --> 00:14:57.470
and authorization on
per application level.

00:14:57.470 --> 00:15:01.610
And this helps you basically
let your employees work

00:15:01.610 --> 00:15:05.510
from anywhere on
untrusted networks.

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:09.140
Cloud Armor-- this is our
DDoS protection engine.

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:12.980
Besides DDoS attacks, it
has protections built-in

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:14.870
against a host of
different attacks,

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:19.730
such as SQL injection,
XSS attacks.

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:22.820
Currently it supports IP
blacklist and whitelist,

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:26.630
and also it has a
custom rules language

00:15:26.630 --> 00:15:31.310
which you can use to
have custom rules set up

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:34.320
for your applications.

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:37.250
If you look here, you basically
have to set up, again,

00:15:37.250 --> 00:15:40.160
in a very similar way to IAP.

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:42.200
You setup a
BackendConfig object,

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:45.890
and you define your security
policy within your spec.

00:15:45.890 --> 00:15:48.650
The security policy
that you define here,

00:15:48.650 --> 00:15:50.630
most of the
configuration is actually

00:15:50.630 --> 00:15:54.140
a part of your regular
Cloud Armor setup.

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:56.900
And what you define
here is only the linkage

00:15:56.900 --> 00:15:59.990
between the name of
your security policy

00:15:59.990 --> 00:16:02.300
that you defined in
your Cloud Armor setup.

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:05.160
And similarly, once you define
your BackendConfig object,

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:11.185
you have to add a link back
to your service definition.

00:16:11.185 --> 00:16:13.010
And this was done on
purpose, because we

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:16.529
wanted to simplify configuration
for your Cloud Armor security

00:16:16.529 --> 00:16:19.070
policies and have them be done
in one single place with Cloud

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:23.760
Armor, and then just add
linkages wherever possible.

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:28.380
Visibility-- so one of
the most important things

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:31.260
when we talk about
network security

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:34.620
is being able to see what
is going on in your network

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:39.810
and let you know if there's any
malicious actors trying to gain

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:41.530
advantage of your network.

00:16:41.530 --> 00:16:45.960
So one of the most important
things that we have for you

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:48.960
would be VPC Flow
Logs that can actually

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:54.060
give you raw logs of all the
flows within your network.

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:57.770
VPC Flow Logs are aggregated
every five seconds,

00:16:57.770 --> 00:17:03.810
or nearly real-time, and they
are not agent-based for GKE.

00:17:03.810 --> 00:17:06.990
VPC Flow Logs, moreover,
cover all the flow

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:09.970
within your network, whether
it's to or from the internet,

00:17:09.970 --> 00:17:13.349
whether it's between
services in your VPC,

00:17:13.349 --> 00:17:16.230
whether it's to manage
services, or whether it's

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:18.210
from your on-premises.

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:21.300
Moreover, it includes
both egress and ingress.

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:24.900
And it can be enabled at
the granularity of a subnet.

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:27.300
It also covers TCP-UDP flows.

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:31.410
And it also covers a host of
features like Shared VPC, VPC

00:17:31.410 --> 00:17:33.680
Peering, et cetera.

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:37.210
A typical flow log record
includes bytes, inferred RTTs.

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:40.030
Moreover, it has
several annotations that

00:17:40.030 --> 00:17:43.080
would be very useful, such
as VPC annotations, which

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:46.260
instance, which
subnetwork, et cetera,

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:50.370
and which region, which zone,
and other Geo annotations.

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:54.240
It can be used for security
analysis, network forensics.

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:58.230
Moreover, you can also
export the raw VPC Flow

00:17:58.230 --> 00:18:02.400
Logs in a secure manner using
things like Cloud Pub/Sub

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:06.990
to Stackdriver Logging, or
BigQuery, or any other engine

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:13.210
of your choosing to visualize
them and track them.

00:18:13.210 --> 00:18:17.710
So here we see how to
actually enable VPC Flow Logs.

00:18:17.710 --> 00:18:20.320
As you can see, when you're
trying to create a subnet

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:24.160
or when you're trying to edit
a subnet within a VPC network,

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:26.800
you get the option of
turning on VPC Flow Logs.

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:32.120
So now we move into the demo.

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:37.860
So I think I mentioned
earlier, while introducing

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:41.400
Shared VPC, that what is
typical of organizations

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:44.460
is you have infra
or platform teams,

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:46.080
and then you have app teams.

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:47.670
And the app teams
are the ones that

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:51.870
actually create an application
that talks to your users

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:53.070
over the internet.

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:54.930
And the infra team
is the bottom there.

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:56.760
It would set up
things like firewalls,

00:18:56.760 --> 00:18:58.920
or permissions, et cetera.

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:01.950
So typically, what you
want is the apps team

00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:04.800
to create clusters, such that
they can talk to the internet

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:07.710
or just be able to
manage those clusters,

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:10.890
and the infra to set
up all those firewalls

00:19:10.890 --> 00:19:13.450
and other rules.

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:17.860
So here, my setup would
include an organization

00:19:17.860 --> 00:19:21.670
which has a host project
called infra-team and a service

00:19:21.670 --> 00:19:23.560
project called apps-team.

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:27.430
The host project has a shared
VPC network called shared-net,

00:19:27.430 --> 00:19:30.610
and it has one subnet
called apps-tier.

00:19:30.610 --> 00:19:32.830
So the way I set
up my project here

00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:37.390
is my clusters for apps-team
would be within the subnetwork

00:19:37.390 --> 00:19:38.500
apps-tier.

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:41.440
And my host project
has already set up

00:19:41.440 --> 00:19:43.600
firewalls and
everything else for me

00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:45.815
to be able to communicate
over the internet.

00:19:45.815 --> 00:19:47.058
Let's see now.

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:54.690
So here you see details
about the Shared VPC

00:19:54.690 --> 00:19:59.220
network in infra-team project.

00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:02.430
So these are my shared
subnets and permissions.

00:20:02.430 --> 00:20:04.710
And as you can see
here, basically,

00:20:04.710 --> 00:20:09.060
a host of service projects
have computer.network

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:10.410
user permissions.

00:20:10.410 --> 00:20:12.600
And these are the only
permissions that they have,

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:16.650
which means my service
projects in my service

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:18.840
project robot
accounts can actually

00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:23.700
use my apps-tier subnet without
needing to have edit or modify

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:24.690
permissions.

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:26.640
You can see here that
the only projects

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:31.580
I've attached to my account
are my apps-team projects.

00:20:31.580 --> 00:20:35.530
Now, let's move on
to actually calling.

00:20:35.530 --> 00:20:38.440
So if I actually move here--

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:41.770
so you see here now, that I
have to create two clusters

00:20:41.770 --> 00:20:42.844
in my apps-team project.

00:20:42.844 --> 00:20:44.260
One is a private
cluster which can

00:20:44.260 --> 00:20:48.190
host a host of backend
services which don't need

00:20:48.190 --> 00:20:49.970
to be on the public internet.

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:54.160
And my hello cluster has the
most amazing app ever built,

00:20:54.160 --> 00:20:58.210
which is saying hello to
Google Cloud webinar viewers.

00:20:58.210 --> 00:21:04.820
So now, I will just try to call
my application again and again.

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:11.120
And here you will see a
host of VPC Flow Logs.

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:15.330
So what I did earlier
was I had enabled

00:21:15.330 --> 00:21:19.550
VPC Flow Log on the apps-tier
and my default subnet.

00:21:19.550 --> 00:21:23.480
And how I actually get to
or export my VPC Flow Logs

00:21:23.480 --> 00:21:25.520
is to through
Stackdriver logging.

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:32.120
So if I come here and I drill
down within my GCP subnetwork

00:21:32.120 --> 00:21:35.570
and filter by only
VPC Flow Logs,

00:21:35.570 --> 00:21:38.960
I get to all these
VPC Flow Logs.

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:42.265
Do note that every single one of
these records, even if I don't

00:21:42.265 --> 00:21:44.390
make any requests, you will
actually see a lot flow

00:21:44.390 --> 00:21:45.380
logs being generated.

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:48.020
Because we try to cover every
single flow in your network,

00:21:48.020 --> 00:21:51.530
even if you're not getting
requests from the internet.

00:21:51.530 --> 00:22:01.240
And if you try to drill down
here for a single request,

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:04.390
you'll actually see the
different annotations

00:22:04.390 --> 00:22:05.740
that are there.

00:22:05.740 --> 00:22:09.550
You see the VPC name, the
subnetwork name, the project

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:10.330
name.

00:22:10.330 --> 00:22:13.330
You see the source
location, and you

00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:15.910
see the connection information.

00:22:15.910 --> 00:22:19.120
If you see, this is the
internal IP address of my node.

00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:21.980
And similarly, that is also
an IP address of the master.

00:22:21.980 --> 00:22:24.070
So you see, for
example, this record

00:22:24.070 --> 00:22:26.650
is the communication between
the node and the master.

00:22:26.650 --> 00:22:29.940
Similarly, you can use
this particular bar

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:32.710
to filter on the basis
of a specific pod.

00:22:32.710 --> 00:22:35.740
Or if I'm making
requests from my host,

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:37.814
I can filter from my
IP address or something

00:22:37.814 --> 00:22:39.730
to actually see communication
between the load

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:42.250
balancer and my servers.

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:47.020
So this way, you can get to
flow logs for your application.

00:22:47.020 --> 00:22:47.710
Moving back--

00:22:51.940 --> 00:22:54.240
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: So that
was an awesome demo, Manjot.

00:22:54.240 --> 00:22:55.360
So today, hopefully
you learned a lot

00:22:55.360 --> 00:22:57.734
about container network security
and some of the controls

00:22:57.734 --> 00:23:00.760
that we have on GKE to help
you secure your network.

00:23:00.760 --> 00:23:02.680
But talking about
container security a bit

00:23:02.680 --> 00:23:05.290
more broadly, why would
you start using containers

00:23:05.290 --> 00:23:07.050
to help you achieve
better security,

00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:08.810
and is that even possible?

00:23:08.810 --> 00:23:11.230
Well, as we really mentioned,
containers are short-lived

00:23:11.230 --> 00:23:12.406
and frequently redeployed.

00:23:12.406 --> 00:23:13.780
So you can actually
be constantly

00:23:13.780 --> 00:23:15.940
patching and updating
your environment.

00:23:15.940 --> 00:23:18.100
You can apply a patch
in your registry

00:23:18.100 --> 00:23:19.690
that then gets
gradually rolled out

00:23:19.690 --> 00:23:22.148
as you would roll out any other
change in your environment.

00:23:22.148 --> 00:23:24.130
That leads to a
very different model

00:23:24.130 --> 00:23:26.710
for how you manage patching,
rather than having maintenance

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:28.090
downtime and
windows in which you

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:29.830
have to do that kind of work.

00:23:29.830 --> 00:23:31.330
Containers are
immutable, so you can

00:23:31.330 --> 00:23:33.187
ensure that your
environment doesn't change.

00:23:33.187 --> 00:23:35.270
What that means is that
if a container does change

00:23:35.270 --> 00:23:37.210
production you know
something's gone wrong,

00:23:37.210 --> 00:23:39.376
and you can then kill that
container and redeploy as

00:23:39.376 --> 00:23:40.370
needed.

00:23:40.370 --> 00:23:43.120
It also means that you have
content addressability,

00:23:43.120 --> 00:23:45.820
so you know exactly what's been
deployed in your environment.

00:23:45.820 --> 00:23:47.440
And then if something
does change--

00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:49.300
if there's new
vulnerability discovered,

00:23:49.300 --> 00:23:50.860
for example-- you
can compare what

00:23:50.860 --> 00:23:52.681
you deployed to
the vulnerability,

00:23:52.681 --> 00:23:54.430
rather than having to
scan your production

00:23:54.430 --> 00:23:55.540
environment directly.

00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:58.060
That saves you a lot of time.

00:23:58.060 --> 00:24:01.180
With the control plane
being managed in a container

00:24:01.180 --> 00:24:02.890
orchestration system,
you can actually

00:24:02.890 --> 00:24:04.742
secure the communications
that happen

00:24:04.742 --> 00:24:06.450
between the control
plane in the services

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:07.430
and between your services.

00:24:07.430 --> 00:24:09.763
So in addition to what Manjot
was just describing today,

00:24:09.763 --> 00:24:13.010
around using network policy,
using VPC Flow Logs, et cetera,

00:24:13.010 --> 00:24:16.180
how your control plane-to-node
communication happens

00:24:16.180 --> 00:24:20.440
is secured and can use
encryption authentication,

00:24:20.440 --> 00:24:21.790
et cetera.

00:24:21.790 --> 00:24:24.100
And then, lastly, in terms
of container security

00:24:24.100 --> 00:24:26.890
here, good security defaults
are one-line changes.

00:24:26.890 --> 00:24:29.260
You can follow a lot
of best practices

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:30.550
by implementing Linux changes.

00:24:30.550 --> 00:24:34.270
Things like enabling seccomp,
or in GKE turning on master

00:24:34.270 --> 00:24:35.191
authorized networks.

00:24:35.191 --> 00:24:36.940
It's a single change
for you and your team

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:38.740
to get that best practice.

00:24:38.740 --> 00:24:41.050
Now, what that means overall
is that running containers

00:24:41.050 --> 00:24:42.610
allows you to adopt
a fundamentally

00:24:42.610 --> 00:24:44.080
different security model.

00:24:44.080 --> 00:24:45.490
You are constantly patching.

00:24:45.490 --> 00:24:47.050
You are constantly redeploying.

00:24:47.050 --> 00:24:49.770
It is that kind of DevSecOps
or continuous security

00:24:49.770 --> 00:24:50.985
that people talk about.

00:24:50.985 --> 00:24:53.110
But you don't necessarily
lose a lot of the control

00:24:53.110 --> 00:24:54.370
you have from before.

00:24:54.370 --> 00:24:58.520
They're just different from
how you were doing it with VMs.

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:01.680
Now, on GKE specifically,
obviously, we

00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:04.230
think we have great security
and great network security here.

00:25:04.230 --> 00:25:06.994
So GKE provides best practices
with security by default.

00:25:06.994 --> 00:25:09.410
There's always going be app
level hardening for you to do,

00:25:09.410 --> 00:25:11.310
but more and more things are
being built into the platform

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:13.351
by default. You can check
out our hardening guide

00:25:13.351 --> 00:25:14.970
online for what
we'd recommend you

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:17.310
do beyond what's already there.

00:25:17.310 --> 00:25:21.870
Things like network policy being
shipped as part of GKE core

00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:24.330
has been true for
several versions now.

00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:26.760
The Kubernetes
dashboard is disabled--

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:29.670
lots of other security
features built-in.

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:32.410
GKE patches you did the
latest version automatically.

00:25:32.410 --> 00:25:36.390
So the control plane is patched
for you by our SRE team,

00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:38.220
and then nodes you
can set to autoupgrade

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:39.287
to get patches as well.

00:25:39.287 --> 00:25:41.370
This keeps you up-to-date
with not only the latest

00:25:41.370 --> 00:25:43.036
feature releases,
including new security

00:25:43.036 --> 00:25:44.640
features the new
networking features,

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:46.084
but latest security patches.

00:25:46.084 --> 00:25:47.500
If you've been
following the news,

00:25:47.500 --> 00:25:49.410
we had a major
security vulnerability

00:25:49.410 --> 00:25:51.000
that was announced yesterday.

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:52.590
It's already patched in GKE.

00:25:52.590 --> 00:25:55.260
We had one a few weeks ago
that affected network policy.

00:25:55.260 --> 00:25:57.170
It's already patched in GKE.

00:25:57.170 --> 00:26:00.240
So you get that benefit by
having a managed solution.

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:03.249
And GKE provides the best of
Google Cloud Security features.

00:26:03.249 --> 00:26:05.790
So there are integrations with
things like IAM audit logging,

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:08.280
but also with Shared
VPC, with VPC Flow Logs,

00:26:08.280 --> 00:26:10.140
with IAP, with lots
of other things

00:26:10.140 --> 00:26:12.720
that you might want to use to
improve your overall security

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:16.210
and network security story.

00:26:16.210 --> 00:26:18.740
There's a few links for you
here to learn a little bit more,

00:26:18.740 --> 00:26:21.700
including an overview of
our network for containers

00:26:21.700 --> 00:26:23.050
and container security.

00:26:23.050 --> 00:26:24.750
But please stay
tuned for a live Q&amp;A,

00:26:24.750 --> 00:26:26.416
and we'll be back in
less than a minute.

00:26:58.130 --> 00:26:58.630
Awesome.

00:26:58.630 --> 00:27:00.940
We're back with the
questions from the audience.

00:27:00.940 --> 00:27:03.070
So, Manjot, the
first one here, does

00:27:03.070 --> 00:27:05.860
network policy work to
restrict pod to pod traffic

00:27:05.860 --> 00:27:07.650
within a node?

00:27:07.650 --> 00:27:09.910
MANJOT PAHWA: So you so you
can setup network policy

00:27:09.910 --> 00:27:12.970
to restrict which
services can talk to which

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:15.740
other services on the
basis of, as I said,

00:27:15.740 --> 00:27:17.840
enabled app selectors
or IP addresses.

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:19.220
And if that
condition holds true,

00:27:19.220 --> 00:27:21.440
then you can restrict
port-to-port traffic,

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:23.697
whether it's within a
node or between nodes.

00:27:23.697 --> 00:27:25.780
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Do I
need to do anything special

00:27:25.780 --> 00:27:28.154
to set it up between nodes,
or does it just kind of work?

00:27:28.154 --> 00:27:30.590
MANJOT PAHWA: It will just
work through the policy agent.

00:27:30.590 --> 00:27:31.605
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: OK.

00:27:31.605 --> 00:27:32.980
The next question
here, do I need

00:27:32.980 --> 00:27:34.354
to use master
authorized networks

00:27:34.354 --> 00:27:35.949
to use private clusters?

00:27:35.949 --> 00:27:37.240
MANJOT PAHWA: The answer is no.

00:27:37.240 --> 00:27:39.550
You can definitely use
master authorized network

00:27:39.550 --> 00:27:42.700
in isolation, with or without
using private clusters.

00:27:42.700 --> 00:27:45.744
We highly recommend using
master authorized networks

00:27:45.744 --> 00:27:46.660
with private clusters.

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:49.300
In fact, another
recommendation I will make

00:27:49.300 --> 00:27:51.100
is, you should probably
think of making

00:27:51.100 --> 00:27:54.230
private clusters the default
for all your clusters,

00:27:54.230 --> 00:27:56.269
unless internet
access is required.

00:27:56.269 --> 00:27:57.810
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
That's a good idea.

00:27:57.810 --> 00:27:59.184
The next one, do
private clusters

00:27:59.184 --> 00:28:02.690
have private IP addresses
for the control plane?

00:28:02.690 --> 00:28:04.550
MANJOT PAHWA: So
there is a private IP

00:28:04.550 --> 00:28:07.370
address and a public IP
address both on the master.

00:28:07.370 --> 00:28:09.330
The private IP address,
as I mentioned,

00:28:09.330 --> 00:28:13.640
is used to communicate
privately with the nodes.

00:28:13.640 --> 00:28:16.850
But there is a public IP
address, which we manage.

00:28:16.850 --> 00:28:23.050
And that we use for our internal
management purposes currently.

00:28:23.050 --> 00:28:25.420
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: How does
Istio work with Kubernetes,

00:28:25.420 --> 00:28:28.160
and what does it protect?

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:30.880
MANJOT PAHWA: Istio definitely
adds a few more features

00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:35.330
on top of Kubernetes for more
defense, in-depth protection.

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:39.660
For example, one of the
things that Istio offers

00:28:39.660 --> 00:28:41.800
is mutual TLS between services.

00:28:41.800 --> 00:28:45.280
So your services can actually
authenticate and authorize

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:46.947
while communicating
with other services.

00:28:46.947 --> 00:28:48.654
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: So
Istio is for service

00:28:48.654 --> 00:28:50.920
to service, but then not
for pod to pod, for example.

00:28:50.920 --> 00:28:51.670
MANJOT PAHWA: Yes.

00:28:51.670 --> 00:28:53.420
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: OK.

00:28:53.420 --> 00:28:55.460
And then, what's the
recommended configuration

00:28:55.460 --> 00:28:58.874
if I want to peer my
on-prem network to GKE?

00:28:58.874 --> 00:29:01.040
MANJOT PAHWA: So we have a
host of different options

00:29:01.040 --> 00:29:03.050
under the Cloud Interconnect.

00:29:03.050 --> 00:29:06.950
Depending on what kind
of reliability you want,

00:29:06.950 --> 00:29:10.070
like how reliable you
want the connection to be

00:29:10.070 --> 00:29:11.720
and things like
that, you can choose

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:13.386
a host of different
products under Cloud

00:29:13.386 --> 00:29:15.570
Interconnect, such as VPN,
dedicated Interconnect,

00:29:15.570 --> 00:29:16.070
et cetera.

00:29:16.070 --> 00:29:16.760
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And
I would set that up

00:29:16.760 --> 00:29:18.176
with a VPC or
something like that?

00:29:18.176 --> 00:29:19.360
MANJOT PAHWA: Yes, yes.

00:29:19.360 --> 00:29:20.650
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: OK.

00:29:20.650 --> 00:29:21.880
Cool.

00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:23.540
So I think that's a wrap for us.

00:29:23.540 --> 00:29:26.950
Please stay tuned for
the next session CETV,

00:29:26.950 --> 00:29:28.780
Google Cloud Networking 103--

00:29:28.780 --> 00:29:30.310
Securing your network.

00:29:30.310 --> 00:29:32.050
Thank you.

